 ],
 "literal": "With black-cored pestle that pounded green flattened rice / having made to sleep near the bund of selected-ear paddy / bright-braceleted women play-house play / like Thondi, my beauty having given back / you take and go, O chief, your oath!",
 "translation": "O chief! Return my beauty, which is like the wealthy town of Thondi, where bright-braceleted women, having laid aside their black-cored pestles that pounded fresh green flattened rice, play with sand-houses near the bunds of paddy fields with choice ears of grain. Take back your empty oath and go!",
 "modern": "பச்சை அவலை இடித்த, கரிய வயிரமுடைய உலக்கையை, சிறந்த கதிர்களைக் கொண்ட நெல்வயலின் வரம்பிலே கிடத்திவிட்டு, ஒளிரும் வளையல்களை அணிந்த பெண்கள் வண்டல் விளையாடும் தொண்டி என்னும் ஊரைப் போன்ற எனது அழகைத் திரும்பத் தந்துவிட்டு, மகிழ்நனே, உனது சபதத்தை நீயே எடுத்துக் கொண்டு போவாயாக!",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine's friend to the hero who has returned from his courtesan's house. He attempts to appease the heroine by making solemn oaths of his loyalty. The friend rejects his advances, demanding that he first return the heroine's lost beauty and youth, which faded due to the pain of his separation. She compares the heroine's former beauty to the prosperous port town of Thondi, where women are so carefree and wealthy that they leave their heavy pestles on the fertile paddy field bunds to play sand games. This rich Marutam landscape serves as a metaphor for the heroine's precious youth, which the hero has neglected and ruined.",
